  "bibliography": {
    "abstract": "This study aims to assess the spatio-temporal dynamics of urban expansion and its influence on urban green and blue spaces (UGBSs) during 1995–2025 in Jabalpur city using geospatial techniques and align UGBSs with sustainable development goals (SDGs) target 11.3. For this Landsat datasets have been utilized for land use and land cover (LU/LC) mapping and extraction of UGBSs using spectral angle mapper (SAM) technique. The landscape fragmentation technique has been applied by calculating spatial metrics to assess the degree of fragmentation within LU/LC types while quadratic regression has been applied to analyze LU/LC and fragmentation relationships. Result shows that the built-up area has increased from 17.99% to 44.64% during 1995–2025 in Jabalpur city while dense vegetation has declined from 46.00% to 25.53%. At the same time, cropland and water bodies have also declined significantly across all fragmentation types while water bodies witnessed a fluctuating trend. The quadrant polynomial regression shows that built-up area correlates with edge and small core landscape fragmentation, vegetation covers with edge and medium core and water bodies with perforated classes. The outcomes of this study may provide crucial insights for developing sustainable urban planning strategies through decentralized planning, GIS-based assessments and informed governance.",
