  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on remote sensing classifications and projection models for 2025 without incorporating ground-level socio-ecological validation or policy implementation data.",
      "what": "Built-up areas in Jabalpur expanded from 17.99% to 44.64% between 1995 and 2025, while dense vegetation dropped from 46.00% to 25.53%. Landsat imagery processed via the spectral angle mapper technique mapped land cover shifts, and landscape metrics paired with quadratic regression revealed that urban growth strongly correlates with edge and small core fragmentation, whereas vegetation shifts mirror edge and medium core patterns.",
      "why": "It quantifies thirty years of blue-green spatial loss and structural fragmentation in a rapidly urbanizing medium-sized Indian city, substituting generic urban growth assumptions with exact spatial metric relationships."
    }
  },
